Caricatures of these polynomials are depicted in \Cref{fig:p} and \Cref{fig:q}. It is now easy to see that the product $r(x)=p(x)\cdot q(x)$ is a (1/3)-error approximation to $\mathsf{ NOR}$ for $\emph{all}$ $x \in \{0,1\}^n$. 
The degree of the constructed polynomial is $\deg(r) \leq \deg(p) + \deg(q) = \tilde{O}(\sqrt{T}+\sqrt{n}) = \tilde{O}(\sqrt{n})$. 

Thus our constructed polynomial, $r$, has degree $\tilde{O}(\sqrt{n})$ and approximates $\mathsf{ NOR}$ to error $1/3$, which is optimal by \Cref{andornor}.

